WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.640
Setting up a local build environment for a mobile app requires downloading massive Android

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.640
SDKs, configuring Gradle and maintaining heavy build machines.

3
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.440
This local dependency creates immediate physical friction in the development process.

4
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.080
EAS Build offers an alternative.

5
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:23.080
It is a cloud-based service provided by Expo that offloads the entire native compilation

6
00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.200
step to their remote servers.

7
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.880
You send your raw project code up to the cloud, and the service returns a fully compiled

8
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.600
Android application that is ready to install or distribute.

9
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.800
To follow along, you need two things.

10
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:41.120
An active Expo account and an existing React Native project open in your code editor.

11
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.000
By removing the need to manage local compilers, the development process becomes faster and

12
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.960
highly repeatable, regardless of the hardware you are working on.

13
00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.840
Start by opening your standard development environment.

14
00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:56.480
Make sure you are at the root directory of your project in your code editor.

15
00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.440
In your terminal, run the EAS Initialization command.

16
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:05.800
This establishes the initial connection between your local project and the Expo cloud servers.

17
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.280
Check your file explorer.

18
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.400
You will see a newly generated file named EAS.json.

19
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.600
This file contains the exact instructions the cloud servers will follow when compiling

20
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.880
your app.

21
00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:23.800
This EAS.json configuration block uses build profiles to define isolated deployment environments.

22
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.080
Typically splits into two paths.

23
00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.160
Development generates an application for testing, and production packages the final release.

24
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:36.400
A single parameter line toggles the output format between an APK and an AAB.

25
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.880
Be sure to double check which profile you are targeting before initiating a build.

26
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.800
Generating a production AAB when you only need a quick testing APK will unnecessarily slow

27
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:47.840
down your testing cycle.

28
00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.720
Because EAS.json defines all these parameters, it acts as the single source of truth for

29
00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.240
the cloud servers.

30
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.360
This guarantees that every build is consistent and reproducible without you having to manually

31
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.720
configure settings each time.

32
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:05.880
With the configuration saved, return to your command line interface.

33
00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.840
The setup is done, and it is time to start the remote compilation.

34
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.960
Run the EAS build command and append the flag that specifies the exact profile you want

35
00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.000
to build, like development or production.

36
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.360
The terminal will pause and prompt you regarding Android KeyStore generation.

37
00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:27.800
A KeyStore is a cryptographic file required by Android to verify the identity of the developer

38
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.760
signing the application.

39
00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.000
Do not try to run manual key tool commands to generate this file yourself.

40
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.840
EAS is built to handle this cryptographic process entirely on its own.

41
00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:45.360
As shown here, EAS securely generates the cryptographic keys, applies them to your raw code package,

42
00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.000
and then stores them safely on their servers for future updates.

43
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.600
Once the KeyStore is handled, your terminal will display a real-time progress bar.

44
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.320
The heavy lifting is now executing on expose cloud servers.

45
00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.280
Delegating this credential management to EAS removes one of the most common points of failure

46
00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:08.080
in Android development, losing or mismatching your signing keys.

47
00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:13.360
Running commands manually is fine for local development, but you can also automate this process using

48
00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.200
a continuous integration pipeline like GitHub Actions.

49
00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:22.360
To allow an external system to trigger a build on your behalf, it needs a way to authenticate

50
00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:26.480
with Expo, without requiring your manual login credentials.

51
00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.440
This requires an EXPO token.

52
00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.320
You can generate this access token securely by running the designated EAS token creation

53
00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.760
command in your local terminal.

54
00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:41.480
Looking at the GitHub repository settings, you navigate to the secrets configuration.

55
00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:46.000
The token is injected here to grant GitHub the necessary build permissions.

56
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.880
By adding this token, the repository becomes an active trigger.

57
00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:55.000
It can now automatically launch EAS builds every time a team member pushes new code.

58
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.760
That can a browser refresh your Expo dashboard interface to confirm that the cloud compilation

59
00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.160
process has finished successfully.

60
00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.560
You will see a final download link provided right on the dashboard, giving you direct access

61
00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.840
to the generated APK or AAB file.

62
00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.800
While EAS handled the initial KeyStore creation automatically, understanding how code signing

63
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.080
actually works, is vital for long-term project maintenance.

64
00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.640
Next, we will cover the underlying mechanics of Android code signing and KeyStore management.

